findings.fix_proposal:
Branch on `response.status()` before falling through to the body parse, e.g.:

match status.as_u16() {
    401 | 403 => return AlephError::authentication("ollama", format!("Ollama rejected the request (HTTP {status}); check any auth header configured for this endpoint")),
    429 => return AlephError::rate_limit(format!("Ollama rate-limited (HTTP {status}): {error_msg}")),
    503 => return AlephError::provider(format!("Ollama is busy or still loading the model (HTTP 503): {error_msg}")),
    _ => { /* existing fallback */ }
}

Local Ollama deployments rarely return 401/429, so the main practical win is the 503 branch — large models can take tens of seconds to load and the current mapping makes that look like a permanent failure.

Wrap the spawned task in a guard that aborts on drop, e.g.:

struct AbortOnDrop(tokio::task::JoinHandle<()>);
impl Drop for AbortOnDrop {
    fn drop(&mut self) { self.0.abort(); }
}

// ...
let server_handle = AbortOnDrop(tokio::spawn(async move { axum::serve(...).await }));

Then replace every `server_handle.abort();` with `let _ = server_handle;` (explicitly consume the guard so Drop runs the abort), or just `drop(server_handle);` at each existing abort site. The guard makes the leak impossible regardless of how the future terminates.

Hoist the default into a named constant and have both call sites use it, e.g.:

/// Default token lifetime when the server omits `expires_in` from its response.
/// `unwrap_or`ing this is fallback behaviour, not a normal case — the helpers
/// below log when they have to.
const DEFAULT_TOKEN_LIFETIME_SECS: u64 = 3600;

// in exchange_code_for_token / refresh:
let expires_in = match token_resp.expires_in {
    Some(s) => s,
    None => {
        tracing::warn!("OAuth token response missing `expires_in`; falling back to {DEFAULT_TOKEN_LIFETIME_SECS}s");
        DEFAULT_TOKEN_LIFETIME_SECS
    }
};

